    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "# Theory of Mind and Emergent Coordination Notebook\n\n> Hands-on Build It and Exercises."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Build It"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "`code/main.py` implements:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- `ToMAgent` \u2014 tracks own beliefs and per-other-agent belief models."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- A cooperative task: three agents must collect three tokens from three boxes; each box can hold one token. Agents cannot communicate; they infer intent from each other's actions."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- Two configurations: `zeroth_order` (no ToM) and `first_order` (ToM with one-level belief model)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- Measurement over 200 randomized trials: completion rate, duplication rate (two agents targeting the same box), average turns to completion."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Run:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "python3 code/main.py"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Expected output: zeroth-order agents duplicate effort at ~35% rate and complete ~60% of trials in 10 turns. First-order ToM agents duplicate at ~5% and complete ~95%. The delta is the measurable coordination effect."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Exercises"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "1. Run `code/main.py`. Confirm first-order ToM reduces duplication rate by ~7x. Does the gap persist when you scale to 5 agents and 5 boxes?\n2. Implement second-order ToM (agent A models what B thinks about C). Does it improve over first-order? On what tasks?\n3. Inject a **hallucination** into the ToM state: randomly flip one belief per turn. How much does this degrade first-order performance?\n4. Read Li et al. (arXiv:2310.10701). Reproduce the \"long-horizon degradation\" finding: as turns grow from 10 to 30, how does your first-order ToM performance change?\n5. Read Riedl 2025 (arXiv:2510.05174). Implement the higher-order synergy statistic on your simulation logs. Is the effect present without the ToM prompt condition?"
      ]
    }
  ],
